from deep1 import d, DEEP

d("vii-2", "Serdica and the right of appeal to Rome",
 "Nicaea's canon 6 confirmed the established rights of Alexandria over Egypt, Libya and Pentapolis, 'since this is customary for the bishop of Rome also', and of Antioch and other provinces. The canon presents these as regional customs. It does not set out a universal hierarchy, which is why both sides of later debates could cite it.",
 "The Council of Serdica (modern Sofia, 343) was called to settle the case of Athanasius and other deposed Nicene bishops. The Eastern bishops withdrew and held a rival meeting, so the canons came from the Western majority. Canons 3–5 (numbering varies) allowed a bishop deposed by his province to appeal to the bishop of Rome, 'honouring the memory of the apostle Peter', who could order a retrial by neighbouring bishops.",
 "These canons were later circulated in the West as if they were canons of Nicaea. In the case of Apiarius, a presbyter deposed in North Africa who appealed to Rome (418–424), Pope Zosimus cited them as Nicene. The African bishops checked copies from Constantinople and Alexandria, found nothing of the kind, and protested against Roman interference in local discipline.",
 "The episode shows two things at once: Rome was already acting as a court of appeal for the West, and other churches did not regard that role as founded on Nicaea. Serdica's canons were later included in Eastern collections too, but Eastern canonists read them as applying within the Western church.")

d("vii-5", "The Pentarchy as an idea and as a reality",
 "'Pentarchy' (rule of five) is the idea that the Church is governed by the five patriarchs of Rome, Constantinople, Alexandria, Antioch and Jerusalem. It was formulated in the sixth century, especially in Justinian's legislation (Novel 131 of 545 ranks Rome first and Constantinople second), and was used again in the iconoclast debates: Theodore the Studite argued that a council lacking the five patriarchs could not be ecumenical.",
 "The ranking had grown step by step. Nicaea (325) recognised the old regional primacies; Constantinople (381) placed the new capital second; Chalcedon (451) gave Jerusalem patriarchal status and, in canon 28, extended Constantinople's jurisdiction, which Rome rejected. The Quinisext Council (692), canon 36, set out the full order of five.",
 "In practice the system was never complete. After 451 the non-Chalcedonian majority in Egypt and much of Syria had its own patriarchs, and the Chalcedonian patriarchs there represented minorities. After the Arab conquests of the 630s–640s the three Eastern patriarchates lived under Muslim rule. The Church of the East, with its own catholicos in Persia, stood outside the scheme altogether.",
 "The theory still mattered. Orthodox writers later used it to argue that the Church is governed collegially and that Rome's primacy was one of honour among equals; Latin writers argued that the other sees derived their authority from Peter. Today the Orthodox world is organised in autocephalous churches, several of them newer patriarchates (Moscow, Serbia, Romania, Bulgaria, Georgia).")

d("viii-1", "When East and West stopped understanding each other",
 "Until about 400 educated Westerners read Greek, and Rome's own liturgy and theology had been Greek-speaking until the third century. By the sixth century this had changed. Augustine read Greek only with difficulty; Gregory the Great, who had lived in Constantinople for years, never learned it. In the East, knowledge of Latin declined after Justinian, though Latin remained the language of law for a time.",
 "Politically, the West broke into barbarian kingdoms after 476, while the East remained a Roman state with an emperor in Constantinople. Justinian's reconquest of Italy (535–554) restored imperial rule for a while, but the Lombard invasion from 568 left only scattered imperial territories, governed from the Exarchate of Ravenna.",
 "From 537 to 752 the papacy lived in the 'Byzantine' period: popes needed imperial confirmation, and between 678 and 752 most were Greek or Syrian by origin. The relationship could be brutal. Pope Martin I was arrested in 653 for opposing Monothelitism, condemned in Constantinople and exiled to Crimea, where he died. Rome remained loyal to the empire in principle but increasingly had to fend for itself.",
 "The decisive change came when Ravenna fell to the Lombards in 751. With no effective imperial protection, Pope Stephen II turned to the Frankish king Pepin. From then on the papacy looked north, and the two halves of Christendom developed their theology, liturgy and law with less and less contact.")

d("viii-2", "The Donation of Constantine and the Carolingian Church",
 "Pepin's donation of 754–756 gave the pope lands in central Italy captured from the Lombards, the core of the Papal States, which lasted until 1870. Around the same time someone, probably in Rome, forged the Donation of Constantine (Constitutum Constantini), which claimed that Constantine, cured of leprosy by Pope Sylvester, had granted the pope authority over Rome and the western provinces and imperial regalia. The forgery was widely accepted in the Middle Ages and was used in papal claims, though not always. Lorenzo Valla demonstrated its falsity in 1440 on linguistic grounds.",
 "Charlemagne (king 768, emperor 800) treated the Church as part of his responsibility as ruler. He promoted the Roman liturgy, Benedictine monasticism, correct Latin texts and schools, an effort historians call the Carolingian renewal. Alcuin of York was his chief adviser on education.",
 "The coronation of Christmas Day 800 was possible partly because the throne at Constantinople was held by a woman, Irene, whom Westerners could treat as leaving it vacant. Byzantium regarded the title as usurped and recognised Charlemagne only as 'emperor' (not 'of the Romans') in 812.",
 "Theological independence followed. The Libri Carolini (c. 790–793) criticised Nicaea II on images, working from a poor Latin translation, and the Synod of Frankfurt (794) rejected the council. Frankish theologians also defended the Filioque, and Charlemagne pressed Pope Leo III to add it to the creed. Leo refused and had the creed without it engraved on silver tablets in St Peter's.")

d("viii-3", "Iconoclasm: causes, arguments and outcome",
 "Byzantine iconoclasm had two periods: about 730–787, under Leo III and especially Constantine V, and 815–843. Traditional dates for Leo's first measures (726 or 730) depend on later, hostile sources, and recent historians think his actions may have been more limited than once claimed. Explanations for the movement include reaction to military disasters seen as divine punishment, the influence of Islamic and Jewish criticism of images, and imperial efforts to control the Church and monasteries. None is decisive on its own.",
 "The iconoclast case, formulated at the Council of Hieria (754), was Christological: an image of Christ either depicts only his humanity, dividing him like Nestorius, or claims to depict his divinity, which cannot be circumscribed and so confuses the natures. The only true image of Christ is the Eucharist.",
 "Defenders of icons, above all John of Damascus (writing in Muslim-ruled Palestine) and later Theodore the Studite and Patriarch Nikephoros, answered that the incarnation itself made God visible, so to refuse to depict Christ is to deny that he became truly human. They distinguished honour or veneration given to images from the worship due to God alone, and argued that honour passes from the image to its prototype.",
 "Nicaea II (787) adopted this position. A second iconoclast period followed military defeats, and the final restoration came in March 843 under the regent Theodora. It is still celebrated in the Orthodox Church as the Triumph of Orthodoxy on the first Sunday of Lent.")

d("viii-4", "Differences of practice and why they mattered",
 "Many disputes between East and West concerned practice rather than doctrine, but in the Middle Ages practice was often seen as an expression of faith. The list compiled by both sides grew over time.",
 "The Quinisext Council of 692 (in Trullo) issued canons meant to complete the fifth and sixth councils. Several criticised Roman customs directly: canon 13 defended married priests against the Roman requirement of continence; canon 55 forbade fasting on Saturdays in Lent, a Roman practice; canon 82 forbade depicting Christ as a lamb. Rome never accepted the council as a whole.",
 "Other differences included the use of leavened bread in the East and unleavened bread (azymes) in the West, which became a major issue in the 1050s; Western clergy were clean-shaven while Eastern clergy wore beards; in the West only bishops confirmed, while in the East priests chrismated infants at baptism; the date and rules of Lent differed; and Eastern churches distributed Communion in both kinds to all, including infants.",
 "In periods of good relations, writers on both sides accepted such diversity, as Pope Gregory I and Photius both did at times. In periods of conflict, lists of 'errors' circulated, mixing serious issues like the Filioque with trivial ones. Modern dialogue has largely returned to the earlier principle that diversity of custom need not break communion.")

d("viii-7", "Estrangement rather than a single break",
 "The idea that East and West drifted apart over centuries, rather than splitting at a single moment, was put most influentially by the Catholic theologian Yves Congar in After Nine Hundred Years (1959). He described a gradual 'estrangement' in which each side lost the habit of living with the other, so that differences that had once been tolerated became intolerable.",
 "On this view the factors described in this volume reinforced one another: language (fewer people could read the other's theology); politics (the Frankish empire and the papal alliance with it); ecclesiology (Rome's growing claims of jurisdiction versus Eastern conciliar and patriarchal structures); theology (the Filioque and different styles of Trinitarian thought); and custom (azymes, celibacy, fasting). None of them alone made schism inevitable.",
 "Moments of open rupture, such as the Photian crisis of 863–867 or the mutual excommunications of 1054, were each followed by restored relations of a kind. What made later separation permanent, most historians agree, was the experience of the Crusades and especially the sack of Constantinople in 1204 (IX.3), which turned a quarrel among clergy into hostility among peoples.")

d("ix-5", "The modern dialogue step by step",
 "The first public sign of change was the meeting of Pope Paul VI and Ecumenical Patriarch Athenagoras I in Jerusalem in January 1964, the first meeting of a pope and an ecumenical patriarch since 1439. On 7 December 1965 they jointly declared that they 'regret' the offensive words and acts of 1054 and 'remove from memory' the excommunications (see the documents library).",
 "A formal Joint International Commission for Theological Dialogue began in 1980. Its main documents are: Munich 1982 on Church, Eucharist and Trinity; Bari 1987 on faith and sacraments; Valamo 1988 on ordained ministry; Balamand 1993, which rejected 'uniatism' as a method of union for the future while recognising the Eastern Catholic Churches' right to exist; Ravenna 2007, which said that primacy and conciliarity are interdependent at every level and that Rome held first place in the first millennium, while leaving its meaning open; and Chieti 2016 on synodality and primacy in the first millennium. Alexandria 2023 addressed synodality and primacy in the second millennium.",
 "The Russian Orthodox Church withdrew from the Ravenna session over a dispute with Constantinople about Estonia, and broke communion with Constantinople in 2018 over Ukraine, which complicates any common Orthodox position.",
 "Popes since John Paul II have described Catholics and Orthodox as 'sister Churches' and spoken of the Church breathing 'with both lungs'. Joint declarations have followed papal visits, including Benedict XVI in 2006, Francis in 2014, and the meeting of Leo XIV and Bartholomew at İznik (Nicaea) for the 1700th anniversary of the council in 2025.")

d("ix-7", "Four ways historians date the schism",
 "1054. The traditional date, used in most textbooks, focuses on the excommunication laid on the altar of Hagia Sophia by Cardinal Humbert and the patriarch's counter-excommunication of the legates. Critics point out that the legates excommunicated the patriarch and his supporters, not the Eastern Church, and that the pope who sent them had already died.",
 "1204. Many historians, including Steven Runciman in The Eastern Schism (1955), argue that the sack of Constantinople by the Fourth Crusade and the installation of Latin patriarchs in Constantinople, Antioch and Jerusalem made the division real for ordinary people. From then on there were rival hierarchies in the same cities.",
 "1484 or 1755. Others look to the formal Orthodox rejection of the Union of Florence by a synod in Constantinople in 1484, or to the decree of 1755 under Patriarch Cyril V requiring Latin converts to be rebaptized, which treated Western sacraments as invalid. On the Catholic side, the growth of Eastern Catholic Churches from 1596 onward was experienced by the Orthodox as an attack.",
 "A process. Most recent scholarship, for example Tia Kolbaba and Henry Chadwick, treats the schism as a long process with no single date, in which relations varied from region to region. In southern Italy, the Crusader states and the Venetian islands, Greeks and Latins lived together under various arrangements for centuries.")

d("x-1", "What the investiture conflict was really about",
 "In the eleventh century kings and great lords routinely chose bishops and abbots and invested them with the ring and staff, symbols of their spiritual office, in return for oaths of loyalty. Bishops were also great landholders and royal servants. Reformers attacked two linked abuses: simony (buying church offices) and clerical marriage (nicolaitism). From the 1050s they increasingly saw lay investiture itself as the root of both.",
 "Gregory VII (1073–1085) pressed the issue furthest. The Dictatus papae (1075), a list of propositions in his register, claimed that the pope alone may use imperial insignia, may depose emperors, and can be judged by no one, and that the Roman Church has never erred. When Henry IV of Germany and his bishops declared Gregory deposed in 1076, Gregory excommunicated Henry and released his subjects from their oaths. Henry's penance at Canossa in January 1077 was a political move to regain support; the conflict resumed, and Henry later installed an antipope.",
 "The Concordat of Worms (1122) between Henry V and Calixtus II was a compromise. Bishops would be elected canonically and invested with ring and staff by the Church, while the emperor would invest them with their lands and temporal rights by the sceptre, in Germany before consecration. Similar settlements were made in England (1107) and France.",
 "The longer consequence was the idea of the Church as a distinct legal body under the pope, separate from secular rule. Some historians, following Harold Berman, see in this the origin of Western legal tradition and of the separation of spiritual and secular spheres.")

d("x-3", "How scholastic theology worked",
 "Scholasticism means the method of the medieval schools: questions were posed, authorities on both sides were set out, and a resolution was reached by reasoned argument. Anselm of Canterbury (d. 1109) described theology as 'faith seeking understanding' and in Why God Became Man argued that the incarnation was necessary to make satisfaction for human sin. Peter Abelard's Sic et non (c. 1120) listed apparently contradictory patristic texts to train students in resolving them.",
 "Peter Lombard's Sentences (c. 1150), a structured collection of patristic opinions in four books, became the standard textbook: every master of theology had to lecture on it until the sixteenth century. Universities grew out of cathedral schools and guilds of masters and students: Bologna (law) and Paris (theology) by about 1200, Oxford soon after.",
 "The recovery of Aristotle's works, through translations from Arabic and Greek in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, along with the commentaries of Avicenna and Averroes, raised the question of how far natural reason could go without revelation. Thomas Aquinas (d. 1274) argued that grace does not destroy nature but perfects it, so reason and faith cannot finally conflict. His Summa theologiae was influential but contested: some of his theses were caught up in the Paris condemnations of 1277.",
 "Later scholastics took different paths. John Duns Scotus (d. 1308) emphasised the freedom of God's will and defended the Immaculate Conception. William of Ockham (d. 1347) rejected real universals, a position called nominalism, and stressed God's absolute power. The 'modern way' (via moderna) influenced by Ockham was the theology in which Luther was trained.")

d("x-4", "The crusades and their victims",
 "Urban II's appeal at Clermont in November 1095 responded to a Byzantine request for help against the Seljuk Turks, but he framed it as an armed pilgrimage to free Jerusalem, with remission of penance for participants. The First Crusade took Jerusalem in July 1099 with a massacre of Muslims and Jews. Four crusader states were founded. Military orders combined monastic vows and fighting: the Templars (c. 1119) and the Hospitallers, originally a hospice in Jerusalem.",
 "Later crusades failed to repeat the success. Edessa fell in 1144, the Second Crusade (1147–1149) failed, Saladin retook Jerusalem after the battle of Hattin (1187), and the Third Crusade (1189–1192) won back only the coast. The Fourth Crusade was diverted to Constantinople in 1204 (IX.3). Acre, the last major crusader city, fell in 1291.",
 "The crusade idea was also turned on other targets: the Reconquista in Spain, pagan peoples around the Baltic, the Cathars of southern France (the Albigensian Crusade from 1209), and political enemies of the papacy in Italy.",
 "For European Jews the crusades brought massacres, beginning in the Rhineland towns of Speyer, Worms and Mainz in 1096 at the hands of crusading bands, despite efforts by some bishops to protect them. The thirteenth century added the blood libel, accusations of host desecration, the distinctive dress required by Lateran IV (1215), burnings of the Talmud (Paris, 1242) and expulsions from England (1290), France (1306) and Spain (1492).")

d("x-5", "How the inquisitions actually operated",
 "'The Inquisition' was not a single institution in the Middle Ages. There were inquisitorial procedures used by bishops and, from the 1230s, papally commissioned inquisitors, mostly Dominicans and Franciscans, operating in particular regions such as Languedoc and northern Italy. The Spanish Inquisition (1478) and the Roman Inquisition (1542) were later, more centralised bodies.",
 "The legal background was the revival of Roman law and canon law. Gratian's Decretum (c. 1140) and the Decretals of Gregory IX (1234) made canon law a university discipline. 'Inquisitorial' procedure meant that a judge investigated on his own initiative instead of waiting for a private accuser. Lucius III's Ad abolendam (1184) required bishops to seek out heretics, and Innocent IV's Ad extirpanda (1252) permitted torture under limits.",
 "Inquisitors' manuals, such as that of Bernard Gui (c. 1324), describe the procedure: a period of grace for voluntary confession, interrogation, and penalties ranging from pilgrimage and wearing crosses to prison. The Church handed over obstinate or relapsed heretics to the secular authorities for execution, usually by burning.",
 "Modern research based on surviving registers shows that most cases ended in penances rather than death; in Bernard Gui's own sentences, for example, a minority were handed over for execution. That qualification does not alter the basic fact that the medieval Church accepted the killing of heretics as legitimate, a position the Catholic Church has since repudiated.")

d("x-6", "Conciliarism: a council above the pope?",
 "The Great Western Schism began in 1378 when the cardinals, having elected Urban VI in Rome under pressure from the crowd, declared the election invalid and chose Clement VII, who returned to Avignon. Europe divided along political lines. A council at Pisa (1409) deposed both popes and elected a third, which only made matters worse.",
 "The Council of Constance (1414–1418), backed by Emperor Sigismund, ended the schism: John XXIII (of the Pisan line) was deposed, Gregory XII (Roman line) resigned after formally convoking the council, Benedict XIII (Avignon) was deposed, and Martin V was elected in 1417. Constance also condemned Wycliffe and burned Jan Hus.",
 "In the decree Haec sancta (1415) Constance declared that a general council holds its power directly from Christ and that everyone, including the pope, must obey it in matters of faith, the ending of the schism and reform. Frequens (1417) required councils to meet regularly. These were the high points of conciliarism, a theory with roots in canon law and in thinkers such as Jean Gerson and Pierre d'Ailly.",
 "The Council of Basel (1431–1449) pushed the theory into open conflict with Eugenius IV, deposed him and elected an antipope, and lost support. Pius II's bull Execrabilis (1460) forbade appeals from the pope to a future council. The status of Haec sancta is still debated by Catholic historians: was it a valid dogmatic decree, or an emergency measure from a council whose legitimacy at that session was itself uncertain? Vatican I (1870) settled the question in favour of papal primacy, but conciliar ideas remained alive in Gallicanism and in some Reformation-era Catholic thought.")

d("x-7", "Wycliffe, Hus and the devotional currents of the late Middle Ages",
 "The Black Death (1347–1351) killed perhaps a third or more of Europe's population. Responses ranged from flagellant processions, condemned by Clement VI, to massacres of Jews accused of poisoning wells; Clement issued bulls defending the Jews. The shortage of clergy and the scale of death affected the Church's pastoral life for generations.",
 "John Wycliffe (d. 1384), an Oxford theologian, argued that dominion depends on grace, that the Church is the body of the predestined rather than the visible hierarchy, that the Bible is the sole supreme authority, and that the doctrine of transubstantiation is philosophically incoherent. His followers produced the first complete English Bible (c. 1380s–1390s). The Lollards were suppressed after the 1401 statute De heretico comburendo and Archbishop Arundel's Constitutions (1409), which banned unlicensed English translations.",
 "In Bohemia Jan Hus (c. 1370–1415) drew on Wycliffe to attack clerical wealth and indulgences. Summoned to Constance under a safe-conduct from Sigismund, he was condemned and burned on 6 July 1415. His followers demanded Communion in both kinds (from which the chalice, calix, gave the name Utraquists or Calixtines) and fought off five crusades. The Compactata of Basel (1436) granted the chalice to the Bohemians. The Unity of the Brethren (Unitas Fratrum), founded in 1457, later became the Moravian Church.",
 "Alongside protest there was intense devotion. The Devotio Moderna, begun by Geert Groote in the Netherlands (d. 1384), created communities of lay Brothers and Sisters of the Common Life and promoted inner piety. Its classic text is the Imitation of Christ, usually attributed to Thomas à Kempis. Confraternities, pilgrimages, relic cults, the rosary and the growth of preaching all show how much late medieval people invested in religion; the Reformation grew out of a vigorous religious culture, not a dead one.")

d("x-8", "Byzantium's last centuries",
 "The Komnenian emperors (1081–1185) restored Byzantine power for a century, but also brought Westerners into the empire as mercenaries and Venetian traders, whose privileges caused resentment. In 1182 Latins in Constantinople were massacred. The sack of 1204 and the Latin Empire (1204–1261) divided Byzantine lands; Greek successor states survived at Nicaea, Epirus and Trebizond, and Nicaea recovered Constantinople in 1261.",
 "The restored Palaiologan state was small and poor, but its culture flourished. The mosaics and frescoes of the Chora monastery (Kariye) in Constantinople date from about 1315–1321. Mount Athos was the spiritual centre of Orthodoxy, linked with monasteries in Serbia, Bulgaria and Rus'.",
 "The hesychast controversy (IX.4) was settled in favour of Gregory Palamas by councils of 1341–1351. Palamas distinguished between God's essence, which is unknowable, and his uncreated energies, in which humans can truly participate, so that monks who saw the 'light of Tabor' saw God himself. This distinction became characteristic of Orthodox theology and is still discussed in dialogue with the West.",
 "Imperial efforts to win Western military aid through church union, at Lyon (1274) and Florence (1439), met strong resistance from clergy and people. Constantinople fell to Mehmed II on 29 May 1453. The last liturgy in Hagia Sophia was celebrated the night before; the church became a mosque.")

d("xi-1", "Indulgences: the theology behind the controversy",
 "An indulgence was a remission of the temporal punishment still due for sins whose guilt had been forgiven in confession. It did not forgive sins as such, though popular preaching often blurred the distinction. The theology rested on the idea of a 'treasury of merits' of Christ and the saints, which the Church could apply to the faithful; Clement VI defined it in the bull Unigenitus (1343). Sixtus IV in 1476 extended indulgences explicitly to souls in purgatory 'by way of suffrage'.",
 "The indulgence that provoked Luther was the one proclaimed in 1515 by Leo X for rebuilding St Peter's in Rome. In parts of Germany it was preached under Albrecht of Brandenburg, who had borrowed heavily from the Fugger bankers to pay for papal permission to hold three bishoprics at once; half the proceeds in his territories went to repay that debt. The Dominican Johann Tetzel preached it near Wittenberg in 1517, though not in electoral Saxony itself, where Frederick the Wise had banned it to protect his own relic collection.",
 "Other conditions favoured a rapid spread of reform: printing, which made Luther the best-selling author in Europe within a few years; humanism, whose call 'back to the sources' produced Erasmus' Greek New Testament (1516); resentment of papal taxation in Germany; and the political structure of the Holy Roman Empire, where princes and city councils could protect preachers against the emperor.")

d("xi-3", "The shape of the Reformed tradition",
 "In Zurich Huldrych Zwingli began preaching through the Gospel of Matthew in 1519. Public disputations in 1523 led the city council to adopt the reform; images were removed and the Mass was replaced by a simple Lord's Supper in 1525. The Marburg Colloquy (1529) between Luther and Zwingli agreed on fourteen of fifteen articles but not on the presence of Christ in the Supper. Zwingli was killed at the battle of Kappel in 1531; Heinrich Bullinger succeeded him.",
 "John Calvin, a French humanist trained in law, published the first edition of the Institutes of the Christian Religion in 1536, expanding it until the final edition of 1559. He settled in Geneva in 1536, was expelled in 1538, and returned in 1541 with his Ecclesiastical Ordinances, which set up four offices (pastors, doctors, elders, deacons) and a consistory to oversee morals. The execution of the antitrinitarian Michael Servetus in 1553, with the support of other Swiss churches, remains the most criticised episode of his career.",
 "Calvin and Bullinger reached a common statement on the Lord's Supper, the Consensus Tigurinus (1549), which helped unite Swiss Protestantism. The Reformed movement then spread through confessions written for each national church: the French Confession (1559), the Scots Confession (1560), the Belgic Confession (1561), the Heidelberg Catechism (1563), the Second Helvetic Confession (1566), and later the Canons of Dort (1619) and the Westminster Confession (1647). Full texts of several of these are in this edition's texts library.",
 "Reformed theology is often reduced to predestination. In fact its distinctive features include the sovereignty of God's grace, a strong emphasis on the covenant, the regulative principle in worship (only what Scripture commands), a spiritual presence of Christ in the Supper, and church discipline by elected elders.")

d("xi-4", "The Radical Reformation in its variety",
 "The first adult baptisms of the Reformation era took place in Zurich on 21 January 1525, when Conrad Grebel baptized George Blaurock, who then baptized others. Zwingli's former allies had concluded that the Church must consist only of believers who chose baptism. The Zurich council made rebaptism punishable, and Felix Manz was drowned in the Limmat in 1527, the first Anabaptist executed by Protestants.",
 "The Schleitheim Confession (1527), drafted under Michael Sattler, set out seven articles: believers' baptism, the ban (church discipline), the breaking of bread among the baptized, separation from the world, pastors chosen by the congregation, refusal of the sword, and refusal of oaths. Sattler was executed that same year by Catholic authorities at Rottenburg.",
 "A very different episode was the Anabaptist kingdom at Münster (1534–1535), where apocalyptic leaders seized the city, introduced community of goods and polygamy, and were crushed by a siege. Münster made 'Anabaptist' a byword for sedition. The former priest Menno Simons (d. 1561) regrouped the peaceful Dutch and North German Anabaptists, who became the Mennonites. The Hutterites in Moravia practised community of goods. The Amish separated from Swiss Mennonites in 1693.",
 "The Radical Reformation also included Spiritualists, such as Caspar Schwenckfeld and Sebastian Franck, who downplayed outward sacraments, and Antitrinitarians, such as the Socinians of Poland (Racovian Catechism, 1605). All of them were persecuted by Catholics and Protestants alike; thousands of Anabaptists were executed in the sixteenth century. Their convictions about voluntary membership and the separation of church and state later passed into Baptist and free-church traditions.")

d("xi-5", "Why the English Reformation is distinctive",
 "Henry VIII's break with Rome was driven by his wish to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon. A series of statutes cut off appeals and payments to Rome, and the Act of Supremacy (1534) declared the king Supreme Head of the Church of England. The monasteries were dissolved between 1536 and 1540, and their lands sold. Henry's doctrinal position remained largely Catholic; the Six Articles (1539) affirmed transubstantiation and clerical celibacy.",
 "Under Edward VI (1547–1553), Archbishop Thomas Cranmer moved the Church in a Protestant direction with the Book of Common Prayer (1549, more Reformed in 1552) and the Forty-Two Articles (1553). Mary I (1553–1558) restored papal authority; about 280 Protestants were burned, including Cranmer.",
 "Elizabeth I's settlement (1559) restored the royal supremacy (now as 'Supreme Governor') and a revised Prayer Book. The Thirty-Nine Articles (1563, English text 1571) set out a moderately Reformed doctrine (see the full text). The Church kept bishops, cathedrals, vestments and a liturgical calendar, which left it open to pressure from Puritans who wanted further reform and from Catholics, who after the papal excommunication of Elizabeth in 1570 were treated as potential traitors.",
 "Richard Hooker's Laws of Ecclesiastical Polity (1594–1597) defended the settlement through Scripture, tradition and reason. The idea that Anglicanism is a 'middle way' (via media) between Rome and Geneva is largely a later, especially nineteenth-century, interpretation; the Elizabethan church saw itself as part of the Reformed Protestant world.")

d("xii-1", "What Trent decided, session by session",
 "The Council of Trent met in three periods: 1545–1547 (moved briefly to Bologna), 1551–1552 and 1562–1563. Attendance was small at first, mostly Italian and Spanish bishops; Protestants who came in 1551–1552 left without any real exchange. The council dealt with doctrine and reform in parallel, at the insistence of Emperor Charles V.",
 "On doctrine it decided: that revelation is contained 'in written books and unwritten traditions', and that the Vulgate is the authentic Latin text for public use (session IV, 1546); on original sin (V); on justification (VI, 1547), its most important decree (see the full text); on the seven sacraments, baptism and confirmation (VII); the Eucharist and transubstantiation (XIII, 1551); penance and anointing (XIV); communion under one kind (XXI); the sacrifice of the Mass (XXII, 1562); orders (XXIII); marriage (XXIV, 1563); and purgatory, saints, images and indulgences (XXV).",
 "On reform it required bishops to reside in their dioceses, banned holding several bishoprics, ordered regular diocesan synods and visitations, required preaching, and directed every diocese to set up a seminary for training priests (session XXIII, canon 18). The decree Tametsi (XXIV) made marriage valid only before a priest and witnesses, ending clandestine marriages.",
 "The council left some tasks to the pope, which produced the Roman Catechism (1566), the reformed Breviary (1568) and Missal (1570), and the Sixto-Clementine Vulgate (1592). The 'Tridentine' Mass of 1570 remained the standard Roman liturgy until 1969–1970.")

d("xii-2", "The new orders and the mystics",
 "Ignatius of Loyola (1491–1556), a Basque soldier converted while recovering from a wound in 1521, wrote the Spiritual Exercises, a four-week programme of meditation aimed at discerning God's will. With companions from the University of Paris, including Francis Xavier and Peter Faber, he founded the Society of Jesus, approved by Paul III in 1540. Jesuits took a fourth vow of obedience to the pope regarding missions, ran hundreds of colleges by 1600, and became prominent as confessors, theologians and missionaries.",
 "Other new orders reflected the same mix of personal holiness and pastoral activity: the Theatines (1524), the Capuchins (1528, a reform of the Franciscans), the Barnabites, the Ursulines (1535, founded by Angela Merici for the education of girls) and Philip Neri's Oratory in Rome (recognised 1575).",
 "In Spain, Teresa of Ávila (1515–1582) reformed the Carmelite order and described the stages of prayer in The Interior Castle (1577). John of the Cross (1542–1591), her collaborator, wrote poems and commentaries on the 'dark night' of the soul. Both were investigated by the Inquisition at times; both were later declared Doctors of the Church.",
 "In France, Francis de Sales (Introduction to the Devout Life, 1609) and Vincent de Paul, who founded the Vincentians and with Louise de Marillac the Daughters of Charity (1633), carried this spirituality to lay people and the poor.")

d("xii-3", "Religious war and the road to Westphalia",
 "Paul III reorganised the Roman Inquisition in 1542 as a congregation of cardinals; Paul IV issued the first Roman Index of Prohibited Books in 1559. In Spain the royal Inquisition, founded in 1478 chiefly against converted Jews suspected of secret Judaism, turned also against Protestants and later against Moriscos.",
 "France fought eight wars of religion between 1562 and 1598. The St Bartholomew's Day massacre (August 1572) began in Paris and spread to provincial cities, killing several thousand Huguenots. Henry IV, a Protestant who converted to Catholicism, issued the Edict of Nantes (1598) granting limited toleration. Louis XIV revoked it in 1685, and around 200,000 Huguenots fled abroad.",
 "In the Empire, the Peace of Augsburg (1555) allowed each prince to choose between Catholicism and Lutheranism for his territory, a principle later summed up as cuius regio, eius religio. It excluded the Reformed. The Thirty Years War (1618–1648) began with a Bohemian Protestant revolt and became a European conflict, in which Catholic France supported Protestant Sweden against the Catholic Habsburgs. Parts of Germany lost a third or more of their population.",
 "The Peace of Westphalia (1648) extended recognition to the Reformed, fixed the confessional map by a 'normal year' (1624), and gave some protection to religious minorities. Innocent X condemned it. Historians often treat 1648 as the moment when religion stopped being the main driver of European wars and the sovereign state became the basic unit of politics.")

d("xii-4", "Catholic missions: methods and controversies",
 "Spanish and Portuguese missions worked under royal patronage (patronato and padroado), by which the crown paid for missions and nominated bishops in return for control. Missionaries arrived with conquest, and the question of how the native peoples should be treated was argued inside the Church. Bartolomé de las Casas, a former colonist turned Dominican, denounced the violence; the Valladolid debate (1550–1551) between him and Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda over whether war against the Indians was just ended without a formal verdict. Paul III's bull Sublimis Deus (1537) declared that the Indians were rational beings who must not be enslaved.",
 "In Asia, the Jesuits developed 'accommodation'. Matteo Ricci (in China from 1583, Beijing 1601) adopted the dress and learning of a Confucian scholar and allowed converts to continue honouring Confucius and their ancestors, which he judged civil rather than religious acts. Roberto de Nobili lived as a Brahmin ascetic in Madurai. Opponents, especially Dominicans and Franciscans, argued that the rites were idolatry. Rome condemned them (1704, 1715, and definitively in 1742); the Kangxi emperor responded by restricting missions. Pius XII relaxed the ban in 1939.",
 "Japan saw rapid growth after Francis Xavier's arrival in 1549 and then fierce persecution: the 26 martyrs of Nagasaki in 1597, a total ban in 1614, and 'hidden Christians' who survived without priests until the 1860s.",
 "In 1622 Gregory XV founded the Congregation for the Propagation of the Faith (Propaganda Fide) to bring missions under papal rather than royal direction and to train local clergy. The Jesuit 'reductions' in Paraguay organised Guaraní communities until the Jesuits' expulsion in 1767.")
